== 4.1 Internal Architecture of the Central Processing Unit
The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the primary silicon integrated circuit that interprets and executes machine code instructions. Internally, every microprocessor is composed of four interdependent functional modules:

#v(2pt)

#analogy-box(title: "The Orchestra Conductor & Master Accountant Analogy")[
  Think of the CPU as a world-class symphony and accounting firm combined:
  - #strong[Control Unit (CU):] The #hl[Orchestra Conductor]. It does not play any instruments itself; instead, it raises its baton, keeps time with the clock, and signals which musician plays at which millisecond.
  - #strong[Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU):] The #hl[Lightning-Fast Calculator]. It only performs math (+, -, \*, /) and binary boolean decisions (AND, OR, NOT).
  - #strong[Registers:] The #hl[Chemist's Test Tubes]. Ultra-fast scratchpads right on the silicon die holding values being worked on this nanosecond.
  - #strong[Bus Interface Unit (BIU):] The #hl[Shipping Dock]. Loads instructions and data from motherboard RAM into CPU caches.
]

