== 4.2 Essential CPU Registers & Their Dedicated Architectural Roles
Registers are the fastest storage locations in any computing system. Unlike RAM, which sits across the external memory bus, registers are fabricated directly on the processor die:

#v(2pt)

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1.4fr, 2.6fr),
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { rtech-navy } else if calc.even(y) { rtech-paper } else { white },
  stroke: 0.3pt + rtech-border,
  inset: 3.5pt,
  table.header([*Register*], [*Full Hardware Name*], [*Dedicated Architectural Responsibility*]),
  [#hl[*PC*]], [Program Counter], [Holds the #hl[memory address of the very next instruction] to be fetched. Automatically increments after every fetch cycle.],
  [#hl[*IR*]], [Instruction Register], [Holds the current binary instruction opcode fetched from RAM while the Control Unit decodes its bitfield.],
  [#hl[*MAR*]], [Memory Address Register], [Connected directly to the motherboard address bus. Holds the RAM address to be read from or written to.],
  [#hl[*MBR / MDR*]], [Memory Buffer / Data Register], [Holds the raw data word fetched from RAM or waiting to be written into physical memory.],
  [#hl[*AC*]], [Accumulator Register], [Stores intermediate arithmetic calculation results generated by the ALU before writing back.],
  [#hl[*Flags / PSW*]], [Program Status Word], [1-bit flip-flop indicators: #strong[Zero Flag (Z)], #strong[Carry Flag (C)], #strong[Sign Flag (S)], and #strong[Overflow Flag (O)].]
)

#v(4pt)

== 4.3 The 4-Stage Machine Instruction Cycle (Fetch - Decode - Execute - Store)
Every instruction executed by a CPU—whether adding two numbers or rendering a game frame—flows through the continuous #hl[Machine Instruction Cycle]:

#v(2pt)

#grid(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 8pt,
  [
    #block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-border, fill: white, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
      #text(weight: "bold", size: 8pt, fill: rtech-navy)[Stage 1: FETCH (T1 - T2 Clock Pulses)]\
      #v(2pt)
      1. CPU copies address from #hl[PC to MAR].\
      2. MAR places address on the external address bus.\
      3. Control Unit asserts the `MEMORY READ` control line.\
      4. RAM returns instruction word across data bus into #hl[MBR].\
      5. Instruction moves from MBR into #hl[IR], and PC increments ($"PC" arrow "PC" + 4$).
    ]
  ],
  [
    #block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-border, fill: white, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
      #text(weight: "bold", size: 8pt, fill: rtech-navy)[Stage 2: DECODE (T3 Clock Pulse)]\
      #v(2pt)
      1. The Control Unit's instruction decoder analyzes the binary opcode in #hl[IR].\
      2. Determines required operation (e.g. `ADD`, `SUB`, `JMP`).\
      3. Evaluates addressing mode and identifies operand registers or memory addresses.\
      4. Generates microcode control signal sequence.
    ]
  ]
)

#v(4pt)

#grid(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 8pt,
  [
    #block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-border, fill: white, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
      #text(weight: "bold", size: 8pt, fill: rtech-navy)[Stage 3: EXECUTE (T4 Clock Pulse)]\
      #v(2pt)
      1. ALU receives operand values from registers or memory.\
      2. ALU internal logic gates perform calculation (e.g. addition: $A + B$).\
      3. Arithmetic status flags (Zero, Carry, Overflow) are updated in the #hl[Flags / PSW register].
    ]
  ],
  [
    #block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-border, fill: white, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
      #text(weight: "bold", size: 8pt, fill: rtech-navy)[Stage 4: STORE / WRITEBACK (T5)]\
      #v(2pt)
      1. The computed result is written back into destination register (e.g. #hl[Accumulator or R1]).\
      2. If instruction specifies a RAM write, value passes through MBR onto data bus.\
      3. Cycle concludes; CPU immediately begins Fetch for the next instruction!
    ]
  ]
)

