== 4.4 Processor Performance Metrics: Clock Speed, IPC & CPI
A frequent student misconception is that "higher Gigahertz always means a faster computer." In reality, true processor throughput is governed by three combined architectural variables:

#v(2pt)

#block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-cyan, fill: rtech-paper, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
  #text(weight: "bold", size: 9pt, fill: rtech-navy)[The Fundamental CPU Performance Equation:]\
  #v(2pt)
  $ text("Total CPU Execution Time") = text("Instruction Count") times text("CPI") times text("Clock Cycle Time") $
  #v(2pt)
  - #strong[Clock Speed (Frequency in GHz):] Number of electrical oscillations produced by the quartz crystal per second ($5.0 text(" GHz") = 5,000,000,000 text(" cycles/sec")$).
  - #strong[CPI (Cycles Per Instruction):] The average number of clock cycles required to execute one machine instruction.
  - #strong[IPC (Instructions Per Cycle):] The inverse of CPI ($"IPC" = 1 / "CPI"$). Modern superscalar architectures execute #hl[3 to 6 instructions per single clock cycle] through out-of-order execution pipelines!
]

#v(4pt)

== 4.5 Modern Multicore Topologies: P-Cores vs E-Cores & Thermal Throttling
Modern computing systems no longer scale performance purely by increasing clock frequency due to the #hl[Dennard Scaling breakdown & Power Wall] (excessive heat generation). Instead, manufacturers deploy hybrid multicore silicon architectures:

#v(2pt)

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1.5fr, 1.5fr),
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { rtech-navy } else if calc.even(y) { rtech-paper } else { white },
  stroke: 0.3pt + rtech-border,
  inset: 4pt,
  table.header([*Core Characteristic*], [*Performance Cores (P-Cores)*], [*Efficiency Cores (E-Cores)*]),
  [#strong[Silicon Micro-architecture]], [Deep out-of-order pipelines, massive branch predictors, large L2 cache.], [Compact in-order/shallow out-of-order execution, low transistor footprint.],
  [#strong[Intended Workloads]], [Gaming, 3D rendering, video encoding, interactive user tasks.], [Background OS telemetry, cloud sync, virus scanning, audio playback.],
  [#strong[Power Consumption]], [High wattage ($15 "W" - 45 "W"$ peak per core).], [Ultra-low power ($2 "W" - 5 "W"$ peak per cluster).],
  [#strong[OS Scheduling Engine]], [Intel Thread Director / ARM scheduler assigns high-priority threads to P-Cores.], [OS automatically delegates low-priority worker threads to E-Cores to save laptop battery.]
)

#v(3pt)

#block(stroke: 0.5pt + rtech-amber, fill: white, radius: 4pt, inset: 6pt)[
  #text(weight: "bold", size: 8pt, fill: rtech-amber.darken(30%))[#emoji.warning Critical Hardware Trap: Thermal Throttling]\
  When a CPU runs intensive calculations, silicon temperature climbs rapidly. If junction temperature exceeds 100°C - 105°C (known as #hl[TjMax]), the CPU automatically cuts clock speed from 5.0 GHz down to 1.5 GHz to prevent permanent silicon meltdown! Never operate a PC with a loose heatsink or dried thermal paste.
]

